z (jump around in bash/zsh)

z is a bash/zsh extension that tracks your most used directories,
based on 'frecency'. After a short learning phase, z will take you to
the most 'frecent' directory that matches ALL of the regexes given
on the command line, in order. For example, z foo bar would match
/foo/bar but not /bar/foo. Frecency is a portmanteau of 'recent' and
'frequency'.

Installation
------------

The package installs the shell integration as:

    /etc/profile.d/z.sh

Login shells normally read this file through /etc/profile. To also enable z
in interactive non-login Bash shells, such as many terminal windows, add this
to ~/.bashrc:

    if ! declare -F _z >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        . /etc/profile.d/z.sh
    fi

If ~/.bash_profile sources ~/.bashrc, no additional configuration is needed
there. Start a new shell, or run:

    . ~/.bashrc

Usage
-----

Use z with one or more search terms:

    z source
    z collab slackbuilds

The terms are matched in order. z changes to the highest-ranked matching
directory. The data file is ~/.z by default and is populated as directories
are visited.

Useful options include:

    z -l term       list matching directories and their scores
    z -r term       select by rank only
    z -t term       select by most recent use
    z -e term       print the selected directory without changing directory
    z -x            remove the current directory from the data file

Completion
----------

Bash completion is installed by z.sh. Tab completion lists matching
directories, but when there are many matches Bash may insert only their
common prefix. Add another search term to narrow the result, for example:

    z collab slackbuilds

The z database is learned over time, so a newly created directory may not be
available until it has been visited from a shell with z enabled.
